

For Question 12, your assembly function needs to:

receive the long long int argument in X0
add 0xfe
store the result in myvalue
return the address of myvalue in X0

A clean Lab02_3d.S using the same kinds of instructions you’ve already covered is:

.section .text

.globl lab02_3d

lab02_3d:
    ADD X0, X0, 0xfe
    ADR X1, myvalue
    STUR X0, [X1, #0]
    ADD X0, X1, XZR
    BR X30

.section .data

myvalue:
    .quad 0

What each line does:

ADD X0, X0, 0xfe

takes the argument already in X0 and adds 0xfe.

ADR X1, myvalue

puts the address of myvalue into X1.

STUR X0, [X1, #0]

stores the 64-bit result at myvalue.

ADD X0, X1, XZR

copies the address from X1 into X0, because X0 is also the register used for a function's return value.

BR X30

returns to main.

For main.c, you need something along these lines:

#include <stdio.h>

extern long long int *lab02_3d(long long int);

int main() {
    long long int *result;

    result = lab02_3d(10);

    printf("Value = %lld\n", *result);

    return 0;
}

If you pass 10, then:

10 + 0xfe
= 10 + 254
= 264

so the output should be approximately:

Value = 264
